#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064023 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/id #uuid "442b883a-d22b-5dda-8f8a-9eead6ae94b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-566" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 2t+3t^2,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=1$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(0) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(0)^{2}+(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^3+t^2+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/id #uuid "aaa51bb9-cde1-5062-ac75-0f35d6af1f53" :question/math-academy-id "e-567" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 1-2t,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=2$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t >0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =(1)-(1)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t) = t-t^2 + 2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-{t}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+2(1)+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 +2t -\\dfrac{7}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f995afe7-724e-5ef1-a928-f1407e6a5677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297717} {:answer/id #uuid "f531d080-f85f-5752-b424-8850ab35296f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297718} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd90584-fc8d-50bb-96eb-f78fedd8cea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297719} {:answer/id #uuid "4d0e0a0c-dbfc-58b6-8260-1eb622b6709f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297720} {:answer/id #uuid "61b0fb92-917f-5071-9363-078fd0810643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61b0fb92-917f-5071-9363-078fd0810643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297721} :answer-field/id #uuid "34c88a64-9e02-5f60-9497-308b7b4648cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "086db544-da02-585a-9178-5af9c52946c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-102993" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{3}}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=-\\frac{1}{2}$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4}{(t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{(1+1)^{2}}+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t+1}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(1)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{2}{1+1}+K \\\\ 1 & =1+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bad7d98-8fe9-51f4-bd5d-80d57d44a118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297724} {:answer/id #uuid "93234760-5894-51fc-b07c-b4dc0d82d94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297725} {:answer/id #uuid "fc37b984-a029-5d49-bf09-c9b10abdefa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297726} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4e5f36-979b-51b7-ba01-5ac1cc5445d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297727} {:answer/id #uuid "50656289-1263-5c63-9155-93f1e8ead703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50656289-1263-5c63-9155-93f1e8ead703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297728} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebc7dc10-47eb-5352-8df0-fb4b3a27ee41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8985ff83-3d60-5f09-b241-0ff8efcd199d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102994" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=\\frac{1}{16}$ and velocity $v=\\frac{1}{4}$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\frac{1}{16}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & =0+C \\\\ K & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d86341d-8326-5991-8bdc-d7da1e9de60c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^4+12t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302657} {:answer/id #uuid "6428026b-b378-5e8e-95af-fae99e6edc9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^4+12t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6428026b-b378-5e8e-95af-fae99e6edc9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^4+12t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302658} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa6d54e-0edc-56d9-b727-a82027acffee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43159458-17f0-5c7e-9f8b-8cea2a10b3e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-351551" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=8{t}^{3}+12,$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=10$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (8{t}^{3}+12)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+12t+C \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}+12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(1)=10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2(1)^{4}+12(1)+C \\\\ 10 & =2+12+C \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=2{t}^{4}+12t-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae61e0c7-5705-5152-8571-c717d9390da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\ln(t)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae61e0c7-5705-5152-8571-c717d9390da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\ln(t)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302661} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4068a7-9b51-5d66-bfa4-b1a30e2e8a3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2a9c65c-7e62-5190-8aae-70c24cf9e9ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-351556" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=\\frac{6}{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=4$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{6}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(1)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+C \\\\ 4 & =6(0)+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f16f387d-edce-54ee-8767-0b82d1c97ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3t}}{9}+\\frac{2t}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289959} {:answer/id #uuid "19414c18-04a3-56f6-849d-f4dca4fd12ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3t}}{9}+\\frac{2t}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f16f387d-edce-54ee-8767-0b82d1c97ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3t}}{9}+\\frac{2t}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289959} :answer-field/id #uuid "d25ac9b8-b806-565e-8499-3284b9f5ebcc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "273d6d87-8669-5f51-96f8-3d528dae9efc" :question/math-academy-id "q-351730" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)={e}^{3t},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{0}+\\frac{2}{3}(0)+K \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{9}+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f73bf087-b07d-5a5e-89ac-8115d1ae3f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t+\\frac{4}{t+2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f73bf087-b07d-5a5e-89ac-8115d1ae3f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t+\\frac{4}{t+2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302664} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c4b1fc2-5fe7-5d88-9fdb-9b9a0e91376e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "abe8be45-2c0b-5637-9cac-f08714688de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-351770" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration \n\n$$\na(t)=\\frac{8}{(t+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{8}{(t+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\int (t+2)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{(t+2)^{-2}}{-2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(t+2)^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{4}{(0+2)^{2}}+C \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{4}{4}+C \\\\ 1 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by \n\n$$\nv(t)=-\\frac{4}{(t+2)^{2}}+2.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{(t+2)^{2}}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4\\int (t+2)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}t+2\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{(t+2)^{-1}}{-1}+2t+K \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{t+2}+2t+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{4}{0+2}+0+K \\\\ 0 & =2+K \\\\ K & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{4}{t+2}+2t-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c1032a6-da52-52d5-9b4d-1622df16969b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}-t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302667} {:answer/id #uuid "01afd4f7-0304-5fb9-ab11-ced9694d65e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}-2t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302668} {:answer/id #uuid "67e73466-9782-50c3-adee-991c86822324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}-2t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302669} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf1191c-72b0-52d7-b8e3-9358d8f0fb33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302670} {:answer/id #uuid "a043f084-3597-50f1-9033-461bb82f56d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=3{t}^{2}-t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67e73466-9782-50c3-adee-991c86822324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}-2t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302669} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9836608-64ac-5f2c-9e5e-1d9f207f3905" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa2039cb-4bb3-5430-9351-2f1ef471a402" :question/math-academy-id "q-4783" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=3{t}^{2}-2,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=-1$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=-1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =(0)^{3}-2(0)+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3}-2t-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b03e6f01-2787-5ba3-b97e-73da9aa7f86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063623} {:answer/id #uuid "b733bc2d-eac0-5e54-92dd-93d059dc3760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063624} {:answer/id #uuid "dd360ec9-21f4-5c8b-8de8-af522c7b3c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063625} {:answer/id #uuid "e95e542e-8f9b-5637-a4db-90f7c8bea670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063626} {:answer/id #uuid "192102b0-c951-54cd-94e7-2a8dce6f617c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "192102b0-c951-54cd-94e7-2a8dce6f617c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 3t^{2} + 2t^{3} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063627} :answer-field/id #uuid "373c48b0-ac00-598b-9c44-85a45cb3d080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d57deba-f2e4-5693-a811-867d6077d98a" :question/math-academy-id "q-7110" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=6t+6{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=2$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6t+6{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43e68991-37f0-543e-a4da-b78b395a8494" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Acceleration, Initial Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b39c3bc0-1a9e-55c4-8fdf-9f5debf1716f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\frac{1}{6}t^{3} - \\frac{1}{15}t^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063665} {:answer/id #uuid "06f3f8a4-b786-5d1a-8925-ef03d9577a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\frac{1}{6}t^{3} - \\frac{1}{15}t^{6} + \\frac{1}{20}t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063666} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb34644-99f8-5f99-8a88-0855f278c9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\frac{1}{6}t^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063667} {:answer/id #uuid "549219d0-7fb0-536f-92fb-cab5839c08ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\frac{1}{3}t^{3} - \\frac{1}{12}t^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b39c3bc0-1a9e-55c4-8fdf-9f5debf1716f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\frac{1}{6}t^{3} - \\frac{1}{15}t^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063665} :answer-field/id #uuid "05068cd0-df4d-53da-968a-f007ba7be0a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd70f669-79b8-5263-ba05-1e9ec153d20c" :question/math-academy-id "q-7226" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=t-2{t}^{4}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=\\frac{1}{10}$ and velocity $v=\\frac{1}{10}$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{2}{5}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{15}{t}^{6}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(1)=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{6}(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{15}(1)^{6}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{15}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{10}+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{15}{t}^{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cbe422-2f6d-5d2f-955c-822d7df72b67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302674} {:answer/id #uuid "34be67e1-f61d-5387-89fc-fc3a2aee1020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302675} {:answer/id #uuid "48eb4c88-b244-56b0-9f5e-be7bdced7153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302676} {:answer/id #uuid "6d25c8e8-803a-59af-998b-96ccee3a4845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302677} {:answer/id #uuid "b05cb132-a872-500c-9bfe-23d8c784684f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d25c8e8-803a-59af-998b-96ccee3a4845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302677} :answer-field/id #uuid "89141635-8ccc-5802-bea9-28a3da0778d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "403c8296-c4b9-5d24-b8e9-75589a7c953f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7287" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}-t}{4},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=-\\frac{1}{6}$ when $t=2,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{{t}^{2}-t}{4})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(2)=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{12}(2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{2}+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{6}+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}]}
